Z||000000||DecadenceR||06-30-2001||12:19 PM||||3 months late I finally had my car in a shape that I could get my rejection sticker. A few of the things they told me about I wasn't aware of, a few I did know. I failed for visible smoke, (didn't even test the emissions because of this) insufficient brake pedal, headlight wobbly (missing a screw, I have it but fogot), and wipers - one speed doesn't work.<BR>But what I'm worried about is the smoke thing. I just recently re-did my head. New springs, valve seals, rocker arms, pushrods, and lifters, and all new gaskets including the head gasket. <BR>Help please?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||06-30-2001||01:10 PM||||You'll have to figure out what's making it smoke,,, rich mix, oil, coolant,,,, Once you know that you can figure what to do about it. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||DecadenceR||06-30-2001||01:20 PM||||it looked like a white smoke. I can't see it in front of my house, but insinde the inspection building it was pretty visible.||||reg||1
Z||000003||West Coast Fiero||06-30-2001||01:22 PM||||white smoke, coolant may be suspect - perhaps there is a blown or miss-aligned head gasket?||||reg||1
Z||000004||DecadenceR||06-30-2001||01:55 PM||||I sure as hell hope not. The head gasket is about a month old. Proper torque, made sure I used gasket tack to prevent it from moving and all.<BR>Wasn't a very fun job...||||reg||1
Z||000005||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-30-2001||02:30 PM||||ouch... one speed dont work on your wipers and they said something about it?<P>I cant remember the last time they even had  tested my wipers during the yearly inspections.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||06-30-2001||03:01 PM||||Actually most head gaskets are to be put on dry. Using tack/sealer on a gasket that's not suposed to have it can cause it to leak.<P>Did you replace the head bolts? 4 cyl head bolts should always be replaced. The original bolts are weak to start with. (The exhaust side ones are a recall item in all 84-87)<P>84 used standard bolts but they are no good. All others came with TTY bolts which should never be reused.<P>Did you check the block for flatness. If the top of the block isn't within spec you'll never get the thing to stay sealed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DecadenceR||06-30-2001||03:16 PM||||The reason I used the tak is because that's what it said to do in the gasket package, the head bolts I used were new, from teh fiero store, torqued to 92 ft/lbs like specified. And no, I didn't check the block for flatness. I did use a scraper and a dremel to clean off all old gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DecadenceR||07-01-2001||02:49 AM||||Tonight I looked at the exhaust when the car was cold - just ran to my house from my friends. The temp wasn't past the first mark yet. And thereis no visible smoke at all.<BR>Today at teh inspection station, the car was pretty warm, near 200 degrees. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||07-01-2001||12:21 PM||||As long as the gasket said the tack is fine then you should not have a problem there.<P>What year motor is this? Only an original 84 motor would use a single torque number for head bolts. All other motors, including any that replaced an original 84 motor during recall etc, use Torque To Yield bolts. Those get torqued to X FP then rotated X degrees.<P>I'd say at this point you need to compression test it and all that.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DecadenceR||07-01-2001||03:38 PM||||This is an 84 motor. Even has the coil that you can't find an aftermarket replacement for....<P>Someone said the thermostat could make it do that. I don't have a stat installed because of the one time the car overheated I took it out to get it home and forgot to put a new one back in. Is this possible?||||reg||1
